\title{cooccure: co-occurrence networks from many kinds of data}
\description{
\code{cooccure} builds co-occurrence networks, in which two items are linked
when they appear in the same transaction: the keywords of one article, the
genres of one film, the actors of one cast, or the states of one learning
session. The network shows which items tend to go together and how a
field, a corpus or a set of sessions is organized around them. The weight
of a link starts as the number of shared transactions; because frequent
items co-occur often by chance alone, it can be normalized for item
frequency by one of eight similarity measures (van Eck & Waltman, 2009),
and each transaction's contribution can be counted fractionally
(Perianes-Rodriguez et al., 2016) or with a position decay. One network can
be built per group (\code{split_by}) or one network pooled over groups
(\code{aggregate_by}).

All of this is done by one function, \code{\link[=cooccurrence]{cooccurrence()}}. It reads delimited
columns, long (item, transaction) tables, binary and one-hot tables, wide
sequences, lists of vectors and raw event logs, with sliding windows over
ordered sequences and eight scalings of the weights, and returns the
network as a tidy edge list.
}
\section{Results}{

The edge list prints with a diagnostic header. \code{\link[=summary.cooccurrence]{summary.cooccurrence()}}
computes network, node and group metrics; \code{\link[=as.data.frame.cooccurrence]{as.data.frame.cooccurrence()}}
returns the edge, node and group tables; \code{\link[=plot.cooccurrence]{plot.cooccurrence()}} draws a
heatmap, a network or the degree distribution.
}

\section{Conversion}{

\code{\link[=as_matrix]{as_matrix()}} returns the weight or count matrix; \code{\link[=as_igraph]{as_igraph()}},
\code{\link[=as_tidygraph]{as_tidygraph()}}, \code{\link[=as_cograph]{as_cograph()}} and \code{\link[=as_netobject]{as_netobject()}} convert to the
graph classes of \pkg{igraph}, \pkg{tidygraph}, \pkg{cograph} and
\pkg{Nestimate}. The network is undirected except for
\code{similarity = "relative"}, which is directed.
}

\section{Data}{

\link{movies}, \link{actors} and \link{actor_genres} are drawn from the IMDb
non-commercial datasets; \link{demo} is a small hand-built cast table.
\code{\link[=launch_app]{launch_app()}} opens an interactive explorer.
}

